@title CI Provider Comparison
@subtitle Replacing Jenkins for the firmware and cloud repos
@author Developer Experience, Ridgeback Robotics

kpi Build minutes | 38,000/month
kpi Engineers | 120
kpi Special needs | ARM + HIL lab | hardware-in-the-loop

We compared GitHub Actions, CircleCI and Buildkite. Some jobs need ARM runners, and the hardware-in-the-loop lab needs agents on our own hardware.

## Comparison
table = Provider,Median build time (min),ARM runners,Self-hosted agents,Monthly cost; GitHub Actions,14.2,Yes,Yes,"$2,860"; CircleCI,11.8,Yes,Yes,"$3,420"; Buildkite,9.6,Via our own agents,Yes (required),"$2,150 + our compute"

## Benchmarks
data bench = pipeline,GitHub Actions,CircleCI,Buildkite; firmware-build,21.4,17.9,13.2; cloud-api-tests,9.8,8.1,7.4; docs-site,3.1,2.7,2.9
chart bar bench x=pipeline unit=min title="Median build time by reference pipeline"

Buildkite is fastest on the two heavy pipelines, cutting firmware-build from 21.4 to 13.2 minutes. On docs-site the three are within half a minute of each other.

## Pros and cons
### GitHub Actions
- Pro: sits next to our code, no extra vendor
- Pro: hosted ARM runners
- Con: slowest on every reference pipeline
### CircleCI
- Pro: hosted ARM runners
- Pro: second-fastest builds
- Con: most expensive at $3,420 a month
### Buildkite
- Pro: fastest median build time
- Pro: self-hosted agents fit the hardware-in-the-loop lab
- Pro: lowest license cost
- Con: we pay for and run our own compute
- Con: we patch the runners

## Recommendation
! **Choose Buildkite.** The hardware-in-the-loop lab needs self-hosted agents anyway, so its agent model costs us little extra and gives the fastest builds.

!! Buildkite moves runner patching onto the DevEx on-call, estimated at 6 hours a month.
